    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["sound-change"],
    "headline": "Mochi shifted from older mochii, historical mochihi; the page compares モモンガ (momonga) for a similar shift.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "/mot͡ɕiː/ → /mot͡ɕi/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "Shift from older mochii, historical mochihi (see below). Now the modern term for a glutinous rice cake."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Compare モモンガ (momonga, “Japanese dwarf flying squirrel”), with a similar shift."
      }
    ],
    "otherReadings": [
      {
        "readings": ["もちい"],
        "quotes": [
          "⟨moti ipi₁⟩ → */motipʲi/ → /mot͡ɕifi/ → /mot͡ɕiwi/ → /mot͡ɕiː/",
          "First attested in the Wamyō Ruijushō (938 CE).",
          "Shift from 糯飯 (mochi-ii), itself a compound of 糯 (mochi, “glutinous rice”) + 飯 (ii, “cooked grains”)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "readings": ["かちん"],
        "quotes": [
          "First attested in Muromachi period texts.",
          "Originally a 女房詞 (nyōbō kotoba, literally “woman's language”) term, derived as a compound of 搗ち (kachi), the 連用形 (ren'yōkei, “stem or continuative form”) of verb 搗つ (katsu, “to pound in a mortar”) + 飯 (ii, “cooked grains”). The final ん (-n) is likely an informal suffix. Listed as cachin in the Nippo Jisho of 1603."
        ]
      },
      {
        "readings": ["あも"],
        "quotes": [
          "/amːot͡ɕi/ → /amːo/ → /amo/",
          "A shift from 餡餅 (anmochi, “red bean-paste mochi”), deriving from baby talk. Appears in the Nippo Jisho of 1603, described as “Palaura de mulheres, 🙰 mininos” (“a women's and children's word”)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
